# What survives when a paper leaves the lab: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, and auditing our own pre-registration

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

{{c_paper_repro}} of the {{c_paper}} paper claims we tested were valid on our hardware, an SO-101 arm with a Jetson Orin NX. The pieces cost €{{rig_eur}} and the compute ${{cost}}. For each claim we ran the cheapest baseline first and committed the pass mark before the test. The failures fall into three classes. Some methods miss the real-time budget on the edge GPU. Some zero-shot judges lose to a trivial signal. Some latent planners lose to the commanded joints. We propose these classes. We also read the commit history of our own gates. For most of them the bar comes before the result. For some the history does not show the order.

## 1. The register

![Fig. 1 — what survived when the papers left the lab: of the {{c_paper}} paper claims, {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} fail; the failures fall into three classes (the clock, zero-shot judges against a trivial signal, learned latents against the commanded joints); our own gates have the bar committed before the result in {{pr_bar_first}} of {{pr_rows}} and the result first in {{pr_result_first}}.](fig/verdicts.png)

Our rig is an SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB. Most of the {{papers}} papers in this study were shown on setups far from it. {{rig_sim}} were shown in simulation and {{rig_gt20k}} on rigs above $20k. {{rig_lt5k}} used a setup under $5k, which is where our rig falls. The appendix table gives the rig of each paper, including the {{rig_5to20k}} in between and the {{rig_unstated}} that does not say.

The register grew out of our own write-ups. Each re-test on this rig was published with its data, and there are {{l0}} so far. This paper collects them in one table and audits how the table was kept.

Here is one entry. An adaptive chunking paper claims that choosing the chunk length from the entropy of sampled chunks follows the task's phases, long in transport and short at the grasp. On our edge GPU one such decision takes {{ex_a_ours}}. The budget is {{ex_a_bar}}. The entry records a fail on this rig. It says nothing about the method on the hardware it was shown on. Next to the verdict, the entry keeps the claim in the paper's words, our pass mark, our measurement and the published rows.

For each claim we first run the cheapest signal the learned model has to beat, on the same rows. For a predictor this is copy-last. For a latent it is the commanded joints. For a judge it is episode length.

We chose the papers for the stack this rig runs (world models, VLA policies, failure monitors, chunking, edge latency), so the failure rate describes this register and not the field. The register file has every paper we have tested and every paper scheduled next, entered before its test.

## 2. Auditing our own pre-registration

A gate's pass mark had to be in version control before its result. Notes and commit dates can be edited by hand. Commit ancestry cannot be changed without rewriting history that others have already cloned. For each gate we find the first commit that contains its bar and the first that contains its result, and compare them (`repro/prereg-check/scripts/prereg_audit.py`, abridged):

```python
def ancestor(a, b):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(RR), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", a, b]).returncode == 0
...
            elif b[0] == res[0]:
                cls = "same-commit"
            elif ancestor(b[0], res[0]):
                cls = "bar-first"
            elif ancestor(res[0], b[0]):
                cls = "result-first"
```

Table 1 gives the classes for the {{pr_rows}} gates that ran and were logged by 2026-10-31. Most are bar-first and {{pr_result_first}} are result-first. A same-commit gate is one where bar and result entered the repository together. It says nothing about which was written first. The log has {{pr_cancelled}} more gates that are not in the table because we did not run them: one was superseded by a later test, and we dropped the other because neither of its outcomes would have changed what we built.

Table 1: Commit-order class of each gate. The audited column is the one the paper uses; the first pass is what text search alone gave on the same gates.

| class | first text pass | audited, 2026-10-31 | audited, today |
|---|---|---|---|
| bar-first | {{v1_bar_first}} | {{pr_bar_first}} | {{pt_bar_first}} |
| same-commit | {{v1_same_commit}} | {{pr_same_commit}} | {{pt_same_commit}} |
| result-first | — | {{pr_result_first}} | {{pt_result_first}} |
| no bar | {{v1_no_bar}} | {{pr_no_bar}} | {{pt_no_bar}} |
| unresolved | {{v1_unresolved}} | — | — |
| gates | {{pr_rows}} | {{pr_rows}} | {{pr_today}} |

The first pass used text search and found {{v1_same_commit}} same-commit gates, which came from batch logging. For each one we went back to the earlier file that holds its bar. {{sc_to_bf}} gates moved to bar-first and {{sc_to_nb}} to no-bar, because their bar cell declares none. {{sc_stay}} stayed same-commit. The audit table in the repository gives the evidence line for every move. We did not re-audit the gates that were already bar-first. The reward-model progress test of 2026-11-01 is still open. Text search calls it same-commit. Its protocol (`{{top_protocol}}`) is older than the log commit.

The early gates mixed point estimates and different intervals. We recomputed {{rc_rows}} of them from stored rows with one statistic, the episode bootstrap ({{rc_resamples}} resamples, percentile 95 % CI). For {{rc_flips}} of them the bar falls inside the interval (Table 2). The colour judge is a {{acc_verdict}} on the point estimate, and its interval does not settle it. The false-intervention threshold was fitted to meet its rate, so that interval is only descriptive. The register keeps the pre-registered point verdicts and marks these three as fragile.

Table 2: The three gates whose verdict flips somewhere inside its 95 % interval.

| gate | n | estimate | 95 % CI | bar | point verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| colour judge (no learning), accuracy | {{acc_n}} | {{acc_est}} % | {{acc_lo}}–{{acc_hi}} % | ≥ {{acc_bar}} % | {{acc_verdict}} |
| oscillation among failed episodes | {{osc_n}} | {{osc_est}} % | {{osc_lo}}–{{osc_hi}} % | ≥ {{osc_bar}} % | {{osc_verdict}} |
| judge threshold, false interventions | {{tau_n}} | {{tau_est}} % | {{tau_lo}}–{{tau_hi}} % | ≤ {{tau_bar}} % | {{tau_verdict}} |

All {{l0}} write-ups rebuild from their published rows. The check for the cold reruns prints:

```text
$ python3 repro/cold-reproduction/l0_check.py
ok  posts: 29 (report 29)
ok  L0 PASS: 29 (report 29)
ok  L1 REPRO: 6 (report 6)
ok  L1 DIFF: 0 (report 0)
ok  L1 classes: {'REPRO': 6, '—': 3, 'SKIP': 8, 'FAIL-ENV': 12}
L0 PASS
```

We ran the cold reruns from a fresh clone with no credentials. {{l1_repro}} gates reran and matched, {{l1_exact}} of them exactly. {{l1_env}} more stop at the world-model environment, which is not published yet, and publishing it would unblock most of them. For the remaining {{l1_skip}} we need a larger GPU, a private checkpoint or the arm, or the write-up leaves out a step. We also checked the register's citations and paper-side fields against the papers' PDFs, and corrected them. The register names {{ps_ids}} arXiv ids. The ids missing from our papers mirror all belong to scheduled tests. One tested paper has no arXiv id, so we could not check it against a PDF.

