## 4. Three failure classes

The adaptive chunking entry of §1 misses the real-time budget on the edge GPU. The method may work on faster hardware. We call this kind of failure class A, the clock.

In the second case a model judged outcomes without training on this rig. We ran a learned reward model as a failure detector and it reached an AUROC of {{ex_b_ours}}. Episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}}. We call judges of success, failure or progress that lose to a trivial signal like this class B. Class C is about learned latents. A world model ranked the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors, against a pass mark of {{ex_c_bar}}, and an action-only baseline reaches {{ex_c_base}}. Class C holds latents used to plan, rank or track progress that lose to the joints, to copy-last or to an action-only baseline.

These examples illustrate the classes and do not count them. The classes are our proposal, drawn from the author's reading of the {{fail_rows}} failed rows.

## 5. Limitations

One operator, the author, ran the rig and labelled the outcomes. Labels were given at rest and not always blind to the arm. Observer bias is therefore the main confound. Where commit order shows it, the pass mark predates the run, so a label could not move the bar. For {{pr_unprov}} of the {{pr_rows}} gates the repository cannot show whether bar or result came first. {{sc_to_bf}} of the bar-first verdicts rest on matching a bar text in an earlier file. Under the bootstrap {{rc_flips}} verdicts are fragile. Cold reruns cover {{l1_repro}} gates so far. The unpublished world-model environment blocks most of the rest.

## 6. Related work

Two works use the same arm to evaluate policies. One is a standardized real-world benchmark that reports a taxonomy of failures and recoveries for VLA policies [@2606.08881]. The other runs a fleet of SO-101 cells and evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. The vla-eval tool runs VLAs across 14 simulation benchmarks [@2603.13966], and a benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method family [@2610.18869]. We do something different. We test the claims behind methods (judges, world models, latency) against thresholds fixed before the run, and we audit our own pre-registration. The closest work is RoboSeed [@2610.40026]. It scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, including reproduction evidence and deployment affordability. The reproduction-evidence score counts five audit milestones: configuration mapping, task records, task coverage, training records and seeds. Its authors state that the score is not a probability that a result is correct. We give one pass or fail per claim on a €{{rig_eur}} rig.

## 7. Conclusion

> **Conclusion.** {{c_paper_repro}} of the {{c_paper}} paper claims we re-tested held. The rig cost €{{rig_eur}} and the compute ${{cost}}. Every row behind the verdicts is published, and every number here can be rebuilt from the repository (`github.com/ravediamond/paper-register-reproducibility`). For {{pr_unprov}} of {{pr_rows}} gates our history does not show the order of bar and result. When we re-test a paper on a cheap rig we run its cheapest baseline on the same rows and commit the threshold before the run.

